ions  the first one is in the comments you  have one question okay  [Laughter]  in the context of  gender-based classifications do you  think it is necessary to improve it from  the intermediate obscurity to the strict  scrutiny uh will you do that  do i think it's necessary  to have levels of scrutiny  because i think  um when we deal with racism we will use  this districts scrutiny so i think we  also need to deal with sexism  there is a need to look with special  suspicion  that any law that disadvantages  a group of people  and especially when those people  are not  proportionately represented in  in legislative decision making or  executive decision making  so  yes i do think that there is a need to  give heightened scrutiny to laws  that disadvantage  any class of people  this we have time for one last question  wow  [Laughter]  my name is john greenberg i'm a student  of chemistry of college so and um i was  just wondering you've had an incredibly  impressive tenure on the court so i was  wondering what decision you were most  proud of and what  most disappointed you  that question  is like asking me which of my six  grandchildren am i  am i most proud of  all of them  i love my job  and every case  is important  so i  i couldn't single out any one case and  say  that's my favorite  i do think a case that has been  mentioned the bmi case  gave me great personal satisfaction even  the title of the case u.s against  virginia  when women first  aspired to become  leaders in the military  west point annapolis air force academy  didn't admit them so litigation was  started  in the 70s against the military  academies it was started by  women who thought they possessed the  necessary qualifications but were  excluded  by the time we get to  the bmi case it's no longer the women  seeking to get in although there were  many  who who didn't  seek admission to bmi it was the united  states government  telling the state of virginia the  government has an anti-discriminat